  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nHot temperatures continue across the area today with high pressure \nstill in control. The leftover disturbance from what was Edouard \nremains northeast of the region of the Southeast Texas. This mid \nlevel disturbance will drift toward our area and linger through the \nweekend. This, combined with increased deep moisture will bring rain \nchances to the area for the first time in a little while. VCT area \nwill have the highest chances with up to 50% Friday through Sunday. \nThat disturbance opens up/washes out later in the weekend, but \nanother wave of tropical moisture will be on its heels keeping the \npotential for enhanced sea breeze activity through the first part of \nnext week. \n\nTemperatures are a bit tricky with the increased cloud cover and \nrain potential. There is a low chance (25%) that high temperatures \nin the Victoria Crossroads don't top 90 degrees tomorrow, but the \nmajority of guidance suggests rain coverage will be limited enough \nto allow temperatures to once again make it into the mid and \npossibly upper 90s. Will stick with the higher temps in the \nforecast, but realize it's not out of the question for it to be \nseveral degrees cooler in that area.  We should drop temps a notch \nthrough for the weekend thanks to the increased moisture. Well timed \nshowers/storms could keep temps into the lower 90s for a few \nlocations. Seasonal temperatures continue into next week.",
